WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.520
- Welcome, everyone

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:13.580
it's to the first seminar
for the spring 2021,

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:20.800
so we have the pleasure of
having Thomas Ginn today

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.030
from the Center for Global Development

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:27.750
and he will be presenting the paper

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:30.680
on "Prisoner or Sanctuary?
An Evaluation of Camps

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.307
for Syrian Refugees".

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:38.290
So Thomas, he's a part of
the migration, displacement

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:43.960
and humanitarian policy team at the CGD

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.550
and his current research
ask how to design policy

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:50.330
and allocate the assistance
to benefit refugees

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:52.423
and host communities alike.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:57.000
His research of the Middle
East examines the trade-off

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.090
of camps and opening schools to refugees.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:04.110
And in randomized controlled
trials in East Africa

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:07.040
he studying how livelihoods
programs can improve

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.833
both economic outcomes
and social cohesion.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:12.513
As a principal investigator

00:01:12.513 --> 00:01:14.500
of the "Let Them Work" initiative,

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.690
he's studying how refugees
labor market access

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:20.230
affect host communities attitudes

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:22.423
and levels of humanitarian assistance.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:28.660
Prior to joining a CGD, Dr.
Ginn worked at the World Bank

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:32.103
on a survey of Syrian refugees in Lebanon,

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:35.300
Jordan and Iraqi Kurdistan

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.850
and with the Innovation for
Poverty Action in Kenya.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:42.580
He received his PhD in economics
from Stanford University

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:43.790
and his bachelors degree

00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:48.050
from the University of North
Carolina at Chapel Hill.

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:50.070
So just before passing to him,

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:54.610
let me just remind you
that we are recording

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:57.710
as you are aware this seminar,

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:02.500
if you have declined to
accept the Yale policy

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:06.290
on recording material, please make sure

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:08.600
that you have your camera turned off

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:13.050
and you cannot ask questions live

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.780
but if you, if you send
them through the "Chat"

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.433
we'll make sure to, to ask them.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.163
So there you go, so Thomas please.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.760
- Great, Teresa, thank you so much,

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.930
thank you to you, to Tarana,
to Mushfiq for inviting me

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:31.020
and for organizing this

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.540
and thank you to everyone
for coming today,

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.510
I'm excited to be here.

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:37.093
So before I get started,

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:41.210
you please interrupt me
with questions at any time.

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:45.660
So we can just do this like econ seminar,

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.770
just to interrupt me
whenever questions come up

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:49.800
or to put them in the "Chat".

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.160
Great, so my name is Thomas
Ginn, I'm a research fellow

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:53.460
at the Center for Global Development

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:57.417
and I'll be talking today about
"Camps for Syrian Refugees".

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:01.920
As I'm sure this audience is, is aware

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.210
about 80 million people are
displaced by conflict worldwide

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:07.760
and at least 10 million
of these displaced people,

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.620
live in official camps or settlements

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.940
that are managed by usually the UN.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:14.520
So the alternative that
I want you to think about

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:17.580
in this talk, is that
otherwise displaced people,

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:20.440
they're still displaced but
they live in towns, cities,

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:23.000
informal settlements, they self settle

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.330
and have to find their own, find and pay

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.300
for their own accommodation

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.410
and consistent with the
humanitarian dialogue,

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:32.130
I'm gonna call all of these people urban

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:35.023
even though a few of
them live in rural areas.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:39.860
So some of the reasons
that that camps exist,

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.410
oftentimes these are negotiations

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:44.230
between the host governments

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:47.980
and the international community,
the main argument being

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:50.580
that host governments
want to isolate refugees

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:54.100
in order to minimize crowd out
in terms of the labor market,

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:58.910
in terms of public services,
in terms of the housing market

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:02.700
and so as a way to, to
keep refugees isolated

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.770
from the host communities.

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:07.850
The nonprofits, humanitarians
also find this efficient

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:10.840
to identify and to distribute
the most vulnerable

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:13.010
for short-run assistance.

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:15.690
But critics of camps rightly note

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:17.830
that it's, makes it much easier

00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:19.580
to impose serious restrictions

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:23.420
on movements violating the
convention for refugees

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:27.580
and human rights, for, for
refugees who are in camp

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:30.080
and also as displacement
is such a long-run event,

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:31.670
in many cases, the worry

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:35.770
is that camps will prevent
long-run self-reliance

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:37.710
for refugees who are in camps.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.610
So given these criticisms, in 2014

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:43.247
the UN High Commissioner
for Refugees, UNHCR,

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:45.950
announced a policy change

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:48.120
that camps would now be the exception,

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:51.200
only a temporary measure after a history

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:55.530
of only kind of serving
refugees who were in camps.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:57.180
This is the question
that I wanna look at here

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:59.110
is, to think about what are the effects

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:01.200
of creating a camp on well-being.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.730
And I'm gonna talk mostly today

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:05.770
about the effects on camp residents

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:06.603
because I think

00:05:06.603 --> 00:05:10.330
that's where we have the
least amount of data for now

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:11.810
but I'm also gonna then extend this

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:14.540
and think about the
effects of creating a camp

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:18.673
on urban refugees and on
host citizens at the end.

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:23.090
There we go.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:24.680
So the setting that I'm gonna look at

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:26.120
is the largest displacement setting

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:28.130
in the world right now, that's Syrians,

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:31.560
I'm gonna be looking at mostly
Syrian refugees in Jordan

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:34.760
and in Northern region of
Iraq, the Kurdistan region

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:38.110
and so here we're looking
at the Za'atari Camp,

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:42.180
a population of about 80,000
people in Northern Jordan

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:47.180
and to illustrate some of the
criticisms from, of camps,

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:50.393
the Affordable Housing
Institute's 2014 report starts out

00:05:50.393 --> 00:05:53.450
that if the road to hell is
paved with good intentions,

00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:55.520
then the newest slum, Za'atari in Jordan,

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:58.160
is a four-lane highway there.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:00.120
And so, I went into this project

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.260
expecting to find negative effects.

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:05.550
I thought it was important
to add measurement to this,

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:06.620
this policy discussion

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:09.540
with new data that I helped to collect

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:10.867
and I've spent time in these camps

00:06:10.867 --> 00:06:14.860
and it's obvious that life
is very difficult and boring.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:16.730
But when I studied the data,

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:19.320
it's also, it's not
easy to form perceptions

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:20.350
on the alternative

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:23.447
because most urban refugees
are less visible to nonprofits

00:06:23.447 --> 00:06:26.210
and to journalists and I
think we want to assume

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:27.150
that they're doing better

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.180
and I think that's where
rigorous data collection

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:32.040
is so important and in
this case, kind of led

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:35.720
to some surprising findings for me.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:38.660
So the research questions
that I'm interested in here,

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:41.530
how does living in a camp
instead of the local community,

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:43.400
affect a variety of outcomes

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:44.610
and I'm gonna start out

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:47.620
with labor market, labor market outcomes,

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:49.330
like wages and employment

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:51.250
and then crucially look at this income

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:52.720
relative to the cost of living.

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:55.270
So refugees in camps don't pay any rent

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:58.220
and that's gonna become an
important part of my story here.

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:01.720
Then of course, I'm gonna
look at all the other factors

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:04.890
that we have data on
that, that inform places

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.730
about how satisfied we are
with the places we live,

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:10.620
things about access to
education, to healthcare,

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:13.450
your social networks,
perceptions of safety

00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:15.780
and then the respondents were asked

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:17.980
about an overall measure of satisfaction

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:20.993
to kind of weight these
criteria as they see fit.

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.880
Then we wanna look at
how does this difference

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:27.180
across locations evolve over time.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.610
So in this case, refugees
have been displaced

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:31.180
when we collected these data,

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:33.210
for on average of about three years,

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:35.690
so that's not speaking
to the long-run effects

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:37.150
that we're most concerned about

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:39.210
but at least gives us some extent

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:43.560
and I'll try to bolster this
with a few more recent surveys

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:45.053
of my findings here.

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:47.967
Then another kind of big
picture way to look at this

00:07:47.967 --> 00:07:48.970
and I'm trying to think

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:50.770
about whether camps are cost-effective.

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:54.644
So would camp residents prefer
the additional aid expenses,

00:07:54.644 --> 00:07:55.630
if camps were more expensive,

00:07:55.630 --> 00:08:00.080
would they prefer that
additional aid in cash?

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:01.550
And whether camps in Jordan

00:08:01.550 --> 00:08:04.060
are generating what economists
call a dead weight loss

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:08.370
or a potential efficiency
gain by these humanitarians

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:09.203
from opening.

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:14.310
The data that I'm gonna use,
we collected in 2015 and 2016

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:19.310
across Lebanon, the Iraqi
Kurdistan region in Northern Iraq

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:21.730
and in Northern Jordan.

00:08:21.730 --> 00:08:24.040
And I'm gonna present the
estimates from Jordan first

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:25.890
because I have cost estimates

00:08:25.890 --> 00:08:28.650
from both the government and
humanitarian organizations

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:31.143
on how much they're spending on refugees.

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:34.700
Dis-aggregated by location, in order

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:39.090
to kinda complete the picture
around cost-effectiveness,

00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:41.090
there's also a larger literature

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:44.320
on the effects of urban
refugees on Jordanians,

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:46.970
so in order to contribute
to that discussion,

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:50.370
I'm also going to, to start with Jordan.

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:53.110
Just to give a very brief
setting of the context,

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:56.690
so 18% of refugees here are choosing

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:58.770
and I intentionally put
that in quotation marks

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:02.810
because some are, have more of a choice

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:05.320
than others for sure,
given the restrictions

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:08.330
and how they've evolved to live in camps.

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:10.130
And it's important also to note here

00:09:10.130 --> 00:09:14.150
that contrary to many, many
other settings that direct aid

00:09:15.441 --> 00:09:18.933
in Jordan was similar for
urban and camp refugees.

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:21.130
So that's gonna be one setting

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:22.690
that I'm gonna find these initial results

00:09:22.690 --> 00:09:25.160
and I'm gonna extend to Iraqi Kurdistan

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:29.360
where there's a different
paradigm for hosting refugees

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:31.750
and see if the results hold there

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:35.040
and the story is, is remarkably similar.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:37.615
And so these data we collected in 2016

00:09:37.615 --> 00:09:40.900
and we asked people to recall
their outcomes from 2010

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:44.853
before the conflict and 2013
wherever they were at the time.

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:49.700
So in order to think about
whether camp refugees are similar

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:52.790
to other camp refugees and
control for those differences,

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:56.900
I'm first gonna take this
rich set of baseline variables

00:09:56.900 --> 00:10:00.120
that we have from 2010
and do everything I can

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:01.660
to flexibly control for these

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:04.620
to find out kind of the most
comparable group to refugees

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:07.420
in camp settings, relative to counterparts

00:10:07.420 --> 00:10:10.853
in urban settings, in
both Jordan and Kurdistan.

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:13.610
The second strategy I'm gonna use

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:15.910
that I'm not gonna talk
very much about today,

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:18.130
I can if you're interested

00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:19.447
but I'm just going to illustrate it here

00:10:19.447 --> 00:10:22.160
and it gives kind of similar results.

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:27.160
So I'm gonna use the
multinational nature of the survey

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:31.840
and compare refugees in Jordan
with refugees in Lebanon,

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:33.890
where for political economy reasons,

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:35.700
Lebanon never opened camps.

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:38.210
And so what I'm gonna
do is I'm gonna take,

00:10:38.210 --> 00:10:41.070
so the first strategy is
going to compare refugees

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:44.460
who are, they moved from
Syria to Jordan here

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.650
and think about different
types of refugees,

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:50.970
some who went to camps and
some who went to the towns

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:53.050
in Jordan and then I'm gonna match them

00:10:53.050 --> 00:10:55.960
based on their 2010
characteristics to refugees

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:58.750
because Lebanon was closer,
who went there instead.

00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:00.900
And so I'm going to look at the difference

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:04.610
between refugees who
are in camps in Jordan,

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:08.080
relative to their matched
baseline counterparts,

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:09.640
who went to Lebanon instead

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:11.630
and look at this difference in outcomes

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:13.130
but of course the differences,

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:16.280
there's cross-country
differences in Lebanon overall

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:20.690
and so in order to net them
out, I'm then going to control

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:22.450
for the differences based on refugees

00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:26.420
who I predict would have
gone to urban areas,

00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:27.690
had they gone to Jordan.

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:30.340
So controlling for these
cross-country differences,

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:32.600
trying to again, estimate the differences

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:34.363
and the effect of living in a camp.

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:37.180
So that's gonna exploit
different variations,

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:38.630
require different assumptions

00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:40.160
and give me very similar estimates

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:43.580
that I'm going to use to
kind of bolster my confidence

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:48.453
that the story that I'm telling
is real in this setting.

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:51.910
- Hey Thomas, before we move on

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:55.430
I've actually gotten confused
about what you're doing

00:11:55.430 --> 00:11:57.480
and this is the, and
maybe this is the problem

00:11:57.480 --> 00:11:58.870
with the interdisciplinary audience

00:11:58.870 --> 00:12:02.690
where sometimes I imagine
the picture might be helpful

00:12:02.690 --> 00:12:03.560
for a lot of people

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:07.283
and for me, like an equation,
might've been more helpful.

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:12.160
So could you, could you
just help us understand?

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:14.010
Okay, or maybe say it in different words

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:16.610
like in the form of estimating equations,

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:19.370
what the kind of that actually is?

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:22.000
- So the main strategy that
I'm talking about today,

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:25.250
is going to be controlling
for selection on observables

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:28.540
and then talking about what
the unobservable selection

00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:31.040
might have been, looking
only within Jordan,

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:33.010
comparing refugees who are in camps

00:12:33.010 --> 00:12:35.053
to refugees who are outside of camps.

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:38.980
Then I've got this second strategy

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:42.130
that exploits different
variation of refugees

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:46.300
who went to Lebanon and
we have this baseline,

00:12:46.300 --> 00:12:48.010
baseline characteristics.

00:12:48.010 --> 00:12:50.330
And so I'm then going to match them

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:55.120
based on their characteristics
before they were displaced

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:58.520
and look at this difference
in differences across Lebanon

00:12:58.520 --> 00:13:01.210
and Jordan, based on whether I predict

00:13:01.210 --> 00:13:02.490
they would go to camps

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:05.660
and then look at that
difference in difference

00:13:05.660 --> 00:13:07.840
by whether they're
actually in camps or not,

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:10.770
to estimate a treatment
effect of being in camps.

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:12.606
- Okay, actually that helps a lot, thanks

00:13:12.606 --> 00:13:16.290
and so when you say, "I'm
looking at a second strategy",

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:17.950
it's not a second separate strategy,

00:13:17.950 --> 00:13:19.301
you are combining the two

00:13:19.301 --> 00:13:21.258
to create the difference in difference?

00:13:21.258 --> 00:13:23.210
- Well, so there are two
different strategies.

00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:25.380
The results that I'm gonna
present mostly today,

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:27.220
are going to just be the, within Jordan...

00:13:27.220 --> 00:13:29.600
- Within Jordan, okay.

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:30.620
