the second largest host
country is, is Lebanon,

00:25:03.230 --> 00:25:04.840
I'm gonna focus mostly on Jordan,

00:25:04.840 --> 00:25:07.260
where there's about 600,000 refugees

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:08.750
and then talk a little bit about Iraq,

00:25:08.750 --> 00:25:13.050
where there's 250,000 mostly
Syrian, Kurdish refugees,

00:25:13.050 --> 00:25:16.030
as well as data that we have
on internally displaced people

00:25:16.030 --> 00:25:19.053
who came from Southern Iraq
into the Kurdistan region.

00:25:21.440 --> 00:25:22.650
In Jordan at this point,

00:25:22.650 --> 00:25:25.380
about one in 14 people is a Syrian refugee

00:25:25.380 --> 00:25:27.310
and that was the second
highest total globally

00:25:27.310 --> 00:25:28.143
at that point,

00:25:29.190 --> 00:25:31.440
at this point again, they do not,

00:25:31.440 --> 00:25:33.830
refugees do not have access
to the formal labor market.

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:37.420
So if you're following the
Jordan compact discussion,

00:25:37.420 --> 00:25:40.390
this changed shortly after the survey

00:25:40.390 --> 00:25:42.803
but it's important to keep in context,

00:25:43.990 --> 00:25:45.790
the institutions at the time,

00:25:45.790 --> 00:25:48.450
which refugees did not have any access

00:25:48.450 --> 00:25:50.320
to the formal labor market.

00:25:50.320 --> 00:25:53.970
As I said before, refugees
are getting direct assistance,

00:25:53.970 --> 00:25:56.940
both camp and urban residents
in terms of food factors,

00:25:56.940 --> 00:26:00.323
in-kind or in terms of cash
and I have data on all of that.

00:26:01.550 --> 00:26:03.170
They're supposed to be
getting free education

00:26:03.170 --> 00:26:04.430
and some free healthcare

00:26:04.430 --> 00:26:06.740
and for the most part,
that's happening in the data

00:26:06.740 --> 00:26:09.497
although expenses are slightly
higher outside of camps

00:26:09.497 --> 00:26:11.060
and then the other comparison

00:26:11.060 --> 00:26:13.833
between limitations on movement
is that camp residents,

00:26:14.730 --> 00:26:16.530
they, there's limitations in both.

00:26:16.530 --> 00:26:19.480
Camp residents need a
permit to leave temporarily

00:26:19.480 --> 00:26:20.620
and so their freedom of movement

00:26:20.620 --> 00:26:22.460
is most definitely restricted.

00:26:22.460 --> 00:26:26.100
But urban residents in this
context also significantly worry

00:26:26.100 --> 00:26:28.930
about arrests over having
the right paperwork

00:26:28.930 --> 00:26:32.750
and also expressed
limitations on their movement

00:26:32.750 --> 00:26:33.943
in just different ways.

00:26:37.040 --> 00:26:40.170
So in Jordan, there's two main camps.

00:26:40.170 --> 00:26:43.740
This is Za'atari, the
largest one of 80,000 people,

00:26:43.740 --> 00:26:46.020
Azraq is the second one with 30,000 people

00:26:46.020 --> 00:26:48.743
and I'll talk more about
the comparisons at the end,

00:26:50.090 --> 00:26:51.860
these are the populations in 2016

00:26:51.860 --> 00:26:55.600
and it's basically stayed
that way to the present day.

00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:59.640
Za'atari was opened in
2012 and at that point,

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:03.180
the Jordanian government
required residents upon arrival

00:27:03.180 --> 00:27:07.750
but this became just very unsustainable,

00:27:07.750 --> 00:27:09.820
given the infrastructure at the time,

00:27:09.820 --> 00:27:12.040
about 200,000 refugees are estimated

00:27:12.040 --> 00:27:13.590
to have been in the camps at some point

00:27:13.590 --> 00:27:17.500
and moved to urban areas
through either formal means,

00:27:17.500 --> 00:27:20.710
there was a bailout policy or an informal,

00:27:20.710 --> 00:27:22.710
just leaving in the middle of the night,

00:27:23.670 --> 00:27:26.990
that was a major story kind of going on

00:27:26.990 --> 00:27:31.990
and responsible for a lot of
the sorting early the crisis.

00:27:33.300 --> 00:27:36.520
62% of employment in the camps

00:27:36.520 --> 00:27:38.120
is going to be through nonprofits.

00:27:38.120 --> 00:27:40.160
So this is gonna be an
important thing to keep in mind,

00:27:40.160 --> 00:27:44.910
we're talking about income for
refugees especially in camps

00:27:44.910 --> 00:27:48.470
and 80% of employment
is, is inside the camps.

00:27:48.470 --> 00:27:51.470
But I think about this
employment through nonprofits

00:27:51.470 --> 00:27:55.540
as comparable in many
ways to public services.

00:27:55.540 --> 00:27:59.360
So these, they're providing some,

00:27:59.360 --> 00:28:01.770
all kinds of different services

00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:05.150
that nonprofits are providing
and when we think about 50%

00:28:05.150 --> 00:28:06.980
almost of the Jordanian population

00:28:06.980 --> 00:28:09.440
is working in the public
sector, in some ways,

00:28:09.440 --> 00:28:12.083
those are analogous for
the refugee population.

00:28:13.550 --> 00:28:16.600
The other thing to keep in
mind here is that at this time,

00:28:16.600 --> 00:28:19.010
Jordan was imposing "Nothing
permanent" restrictions

00:28:19.010 --> 00:28:21.640
on the camps, so they
couldn't build with concrete,

00:28:21.640 --> 00:28:23.000
they couldn't really invest

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:26.480
in the things like water,
infrastructure, waste management

00:28:26.480 --> 00:28:29.210
that was mentioned earlier.

00:28:29.210 --> 00:28:32.370
And so these are kind of like lower bounds

00:28:32.370 --> 00:28:37.370
for what a camp could be
if there was more openness

00:28:38.630 --> 00:28:41.313
to making these a longer term investment.

00:28:43.710 --> 00:28:44.790
The data that I'm gonna use,

00:28:44.790 --> 00:28:47.347
I collected in partnership
with the World Bank, UNHCR

00:28:47.347 --> 00:28:50.250
and the Danish aid organizations, Danida,

00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:52.670
in the first stage we
randomly selected areas

00:28:52.670 --> 00:28:55.410
and then stratified by
the percentage of Syrians

00:28:55.410 --> 00:28:56.500
and then in the second stage,

00:28:56.500 --> 00:28:58.080
we randomly selected households

00:28:58.080 --> 00:29:01.450
and then selected a mix
of hosts and Syrians

00:29:01.450 --> 00:29:04.423
in each one of these small neighborhoods.

00:29:05.810 --> 00:29:08.330
This is mostly going to be
representative for Jordan,

00:29:08.330 --> 00:29:09.620
it's not quite representative,

00:29:09.620 --> 00:29:10.980
it's only in certain governorates

00:29:10.980 --> 00:29:14.760
and there was an additional
sample that we surveyed

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:18.140
in order to bolster
the, the sample in Amman

00:29:18.140 --> 00:29:20.410
but it's going to include
unregistered refugees

00:29:20.410 --> 00:29:23.470
and I'm mostly gonna be
using the sampling weights

00:29:23.470 --> 00:29:26.358
that we, that we calculated.

00:29:26.358 --> 00:29:28.880
We had a response rate above 85%,

00:29:28.880 --> 00:29:31.500
so I think we have most,

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:33.700
mostly attrition is not much of a worry,

00:29:33.700 --> 00:29:35.460
I think we can call these
mostly representative

00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:37.790
and it's consistent with other surveys

00:29:37.790 --> 00:29:40.060
that have been done in Jordan as well.

00:29:40.060 --> 00:29:42.960
I'm gonna talk about some market migration

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:44.530
and attitude questions as well

00:29:44.530 --> 00:29:48.680
and this was done through, we
sampled two 15 to 64-year-olds

00:29:48.680 --> 00:29:50.550
randomly in each household

00:29:50.550 --> 00:29:53.990
and so it's not the whole
household that we have that for.

00:29:53.990 --> 00:29:55.520
And as before I said

00:29:55.520 --> 00:29:58.700
that we have, the survey was done in 2016

00:29:58.700 --> 00:30:00.380
and I'm gonna have outcomes,

00:30:00.380 --> 00:30:03.000
we asked them to recall
from September, 2010

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:06.103
before the conflict and September, 2013.

00:30:08.040 --> 00:30:10.113
So first look at these data here.

00:30:11.540 --> 00:30:14.210
Again, as we kind of
said and as consistent

00:30:14.210 --> 00:30:15.170
with a lot of the discussion,

00:30:15.170 --> 00:30:17.190
urban refugees in Jordan earn much more.

00:30:17.190 --> 00:30:19.740
So in the capital city
Amman, they're earning

00:30:19.740 --> 00:30:22.703
almost 300 US dollars
per household per month,

00:30:23.840 --> 00:30:26.100
when we zoom in on just
the two governorates

00:30:26.100 --> 00:30:28.310
that are around the
camps, Mafraq and Zarqa,

00:30:28.310 --> 00:30:30.580
that's, it's still more than in the camps

00:30:30.580 --> 00:30:35.050
but it's, it's much less
difference between the camps,

00:30:35.050 --> 00:30:38.750
so about $110 relative to about $80

00:30:38.750 --> 00:30:41.833
per household per month for camps.

00:30:43.200 --> 00:30:44.670
- Hi, Thomas, I'm sorry to interrupt you,

00:30:44.670 --> 00:30:46.830
I wonder if you could give us,
I know this isn't shown here

00:30:46.830 --> 00:30:49.120
and maybe the sample's
not super large enough

00:30:49.120 --> 00:30:50.130
to get into the details

00:30:50.130 --> 00:30:52.440
but what do you know
about the distribution

00:30:52.440 --> 00:30:54.630
of household income across these places?

00:30:54.630 --> 00:30:55.493
- Yeah, so I'll talk a
little bit about that,

00:30:55.493 --> 00:31:00.493
I mean, so 40% of
households in camps are...

00:31:01.180 --> 00:31:05.670
40% of households outside of
camps are not earning anything

00:31:05.670 --> 00:31:10.260
and 60% of households in camps
are not earning anything.

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:11.473
So it's...
- Sure.

00:31:11.473 --> 00:31:14.360
- There's a large percentage
that have no earnings

00:31:14.360 --> 00:31:16.180
from either wage or self-employment

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:20.010
and so it's, it's very kind of,

00:31:20.010 --> 00:31:23.653
yeah, right skewed in that,
in that sense, for sure.

00:31:23.653 --> 00:31:25.270
Does that answer your question?

00:31:25.270 --> 00:31:27.210
- Yeah, and a little bit,
a little bit different

00:31:27.210 --> 00:31:29.440
across places too, it sounds like.

00:31:29.440 --> 00:31:31.440
- Yes, I mean the, the furthest right tail

00:31:31.440 --> 00:31:32.590
is definitely in Amman

00:31:32.590 --> 00:31:36.740
but that's, there's not
that many refugees earning

00:31:36.740 --> 00:31:39.730
a substantial amount of money.

00:31:39.730 --> 00:31:41.920
- Got it, okay, thanks.

00:31:41.920 --> 00:31:44.860
- Great, so then I'm gonna add the red bar

00:31:44.860 --> 00:31:47.660
of remittances and pensions
and any other income

00:31:47.660 --> 00:31:50.240
that's coming in, that's
a very small proportion

00:31:50.240 --> 00:31:53.340
of the reported income for refugees here

00:31:53.340 --> 00:31:54.680
and then the assistance,

00:31:54.680 --> 00:31:57.980
again as I said, it's
similar across locations

00:31:57.980 --> 00:32:01.270
in terms of how refugees
report the value of this,

00:32:01.270 --> 00:32:04.970
so it's the largest
proportion in terms of camps,

00:32:04.970 --> 00:32:08.220
camp residents but a substantial amount

00:32:08.220 --> 00:32:12.480
for off the refugees in the
other governorates around camps

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:13.510
and also with...

00:32:15.480 --> 00:32:18.130
- Sorry, Thomas, the
type of aid they receive,

00:32:18.130 --> 00:32:19.070
is it similar,

00:32:19.070 --> 00:32:22.510
do they receive perhaps
primarily food in camps,

00:32:22.510 --> 00:32:25.140
something else like
services outside of camps

00:32:25.140 --> 00:32:27.293
or how similar it is in terms of...?

00:32:27.293 --> 00:32:29.320
- I think, I think it's fairly similar,

00:32:29.320 --> 00:32:31.860
we don't have, we don't have, we have data

00:32:31.860 --> 00:32:36.260
on whether they receive food
and cash but not the amounts

00:32:36.260 --> 00:32:39.220
and then a total amount
for the total value,

00:32:39.220 --> 00:32:42.810
so I think there's probably
a little bit more cash

00:32:42.810 --> 00:32:47.810
outside of camps than
inside of camps or yes,

00:32:48.164 --> 00:32:51.940
but both refugees outside
of camps and inside of camps

00:32:51.940 --> 00:32:55.110
are receiving food in-kind, food vouchers,

00:32:55.110 --> 00:33:00.110
all the kind of same, same
general bundles of assistance.

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:02.783
- All right, thanks.

00:33:02.783 --> 00:33:05.260
(Thomas whispering)

00:33:05.260 --> 00:33:10.123
- So now when we look at the
rent that refugees are paying,

00:33:11.560 --> 00:33:15.010
we, when you look at the
governorates around camps,

00:33:15.010 --> 00:33:18.200
they're actually paying
the middle set of columns,

00:33:18.200 --> 00:33:21.620
they're actually paying
more outside of camps

00:33:21.620 --> 00:33:23.730
in these governorates
than they're earning.

00:33:23.730 --> 00:33:26.340
So with assistance on average,

00:33:26.340 --> 00:33:28.470
they're, they still have
kind of net positives

00:33:28.470 --> 00:33:31.400
for other expenses but they're
actually paying more in rent

00:33:31.400 --> 00:33:33.150
than their total earnings.

00:33:33.150 --> 00:33:35.090
In camps, they're obviously
not paying anything

00:33:35.090 --> 00:33:38.800
and then in Amman, this, the
total rent is almost as much

00:33:38.800 --> 00:33:39.750
as they're earning

00:33:40.890 --> 00:33:45.520
but a little bit less, a
little bit larger difference

00:33:45.520 --> 00:33:46.983
for residents in Amman.

00:33:49.310 --> 00:33:50.980
So then the story I'm gonna be telling,

00:33:50.980 --> 00:33:53.100
is one about this, this
net difference here.

00:33:53.100 --> 00:33:57.360
So again, you kind of subtract
zero from the total income

00:33:57.360 --> 00:34:00.660
for camps and then
subtract out the, the rent

00:34:00.660 --> 00:34:02.480
for the governorates around camps

00:34:02.480 --> 00:34:07.480
and you can see that this
is, in camps this is higher

00:34:07.820 --> 00:34:11.600
for these residents by
not an enormous amount

00:34:11.600 --> 00:34:15.210
but a, a noticeable amount
in these just raw data

00:34:15.210 --> 00:34:18.250
and so then the next step
is going to be saying,

00:34:18.250 --> 00:34:20.120
Well, what about kind of both observable

00:34:20.120 --> 00:34:23.120
and unobservable differences
that we wanna think about

00:34:23.120 --> 00:34:24.623
on all of these dimensions?

00:34:27.760 --> 00:34:29.580
The last thing I'll just note here,

00:34:29.580 --> 00:34:34.340
is that when we compare
these two Jordanians,

00:34:34.340 --> 00:34:35.670
the red dot here

00:34:35.670 --> 00:34:37.850
is the total household
income of Jordanians,

00:34:37.850 --> 00:34:40.190
we also have in the survey, you
can see that they're earning

00:34:40.190 --> 00:34:43.710
both in these two other
governorates around the camps

00:34:43.710 --> 00:34:47.070
and in Amman, earning substantially more

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:52.070
but the average rent that they're
paying is almost identical

00:34:52.170 --> 00:34:54.600
for Jordanians and refugees

00:34:56.582 --> 00:34:57.700
in urban areas.

00:34:57.700 --> 00:35:01.250
So this kind of net of
expenses is much different

00:35:01.250 --> 00:35:05.820
for Jordanians than it is
for Syrians in this setting.

00:35:10.010 --> 00:35:11.090
So now we wanna think

00:35:11.090 --> 00:35:14.450
about whether, the goal
that we have in mind here

00:35:14.450 --> 00:35:17.130
is to estimate how camp
residents would have done

00:35:17.130 --> 00:35:18.160
outside camp, right?

00:35:18.160 --> 00:35:21.610
Is this net kind of a real effect,

00:35:21.610 --> 00:35:24.360
are these, what would the
wage effects, the wage penalty

00:35:24.360 --> 00:35:28.980
of living in a camp be if refugees live,

00:35:28.980 --> 00:35:32.000
if the camp residents
live outside of camps?

00:35:32.000 --> 00:35:33.920
So for the economists,
I wanna think about this

00:35:33.920 --> 00:35:36.830
as like the Treatment
on the Treated estimate.

00:35:36.830 --> 00:35:39.690
So not necessarily if
you wanna match refugees

00:35:39.690 --> 00:35:42.840
who were in camps to
the most similar people

00:35:42.840 --> 00:35:46.990
outside of camps, to get
just that counterfactual

00:35:46.990 --> 00:35:49.240
and not the kind of
average treatment effect.

00:35:50.831 --> 00:35:52.750
And then we also wanna think about this

00:35:52.750 --> 00:35:54.900
as the partial equilibrium.

00:35:54.900 --> 00:35:58.150
So instead of camps closing completely,

00:35:58.150 --> 00:36:01.210
we wanna think about this first exercise

00:36:01.210 --> 00:36:04.680
as just one resident is
going to be leaving the camps

00:36:06.280 --> 00:36:09.580
and so that, that marginal
effect, that partial effect

00:36:09.580 --> 00:36:12.950
but the, but the camps still remain open

00:36:12.950 --> 00:36:16.143
in terms of all the other
kind of price and wage effects

00:36:16.143 --> 00:36:19.563
that that could have and I'll
come back to this at the end.

00:36:20.490 --> 00:36:24.870
So the very simple classic
identifying assumption here

00:36:24.870 --> 00:36:27.700
is that conditional on this
rich set of observables

00:36:27.700 --> 00:36:31.780
that we have, the choice to
live in a camp is independent

00:36:31.780 --> 00:36:33.677
of the potential outcomes

00:36:33.677 --> 00:36:35.890
and specifically to the labor market

00:36:35.890 --> 00:36:39.800
that I'm gonna be measuring to start with

00:36:39.800 --> 00:36:43.420
and I will go through
the selection process

00:36:43.420 --> 00:36:46.670
that I think makes that
potentially plausible

00:36:48.390 --> 00:36:52.780
and then again, kind of the
second identification strategy

00:36:52.780 --> 00:36:53.920
gives me more confidence

00:36:53.920 --> 00:36:58.920
that this is in, in a reasonable
benchmark to go from here.

00:37:00.400 --> 00:37:03.630
So I'm gonna have these
85 baseline covariates,

00:37:03.630 --> 00:37:06.000
it's things about the
household demographics,

00:37:06.000 --> 00:37:08.850
about their housing
characteristics in 2010

00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:12.170
before displacement, 17 different assets,

00:37:12.170 --> 00:37:15.680
their exposure to violence,
the amount of time

00:37:15.680 --> 00:37:18.560
that they've been in Jordan,
obviously things like that

00:37:18.560 --> 00:37:21.350
and then at the individual
level, again for a subset,

00:37:21.350 --> 00:37:25.330
we have the 2010 outcomes
on employment status,

00:37:25.330 --> 00:37:29.483
on occupations, on wages,
on their education levels,

00:37:31.030 --> 00:37:32.460
all of these kinds of, of things

00:37:32.460 --> 00:37:37.460
that feed into this,
this estimation strategy.

00:37:37.870 --> 00:37:41.780
And so then the location choice
is one I'll be talking about

00:37:41.780 --> 00:37:45.940
both in terms of estimating
this effect of living in a camp

00:37:45.940 --> 00:37:48.250
but also I think it's just
interesting on its own

00:37:48.250 --> 00:37:51.960
that there's this perception
that the vulnerable; the poor,

00:37:51.960 --> 00:37:55.230
more women and children are
the ones who go to camps

00:37:55.230 --> 00:37:57.060
and I'm gonna find some evidence for that

00:37:57.060 --> 00:38:00.783
but not as kind of clear as I
originally, as I had expected.

00:38:03.840 --> 00:38:06.470
So again, consistent with it being

00:38:06.470 --> 00:38:11.320
kind of a more, more vulnerable
ex-ante who would have moved

00:38:11.320 --> 00:38:13.560
and who would have stayed in camps,

00:38:13.560 --> 00:38:16.300
I'm gonna find the camp
residents have a few,

00:38:16.300 --> 00:38:19.750
had fewer rooms in 2010 in their house,

00:38:19.750 --> 00:38:22.980
they had less likely
to have a water heater,

00:38:22.980 --> 00:38:24.250
less likely to live in apartment,

00:38:24.250 --> 00:38:25.730
less like they have a tiled floor,

00:38:25.730 --> 00:38:29.030
so everything here to the
left of the red line, the zero

00:38:29.030 --> 00:38:31.840
means that camp residents
were kind of less likely

00:38:31.840 --> 00:38:33.750
to have these things.

00:38:33.750 --> 00:38:37.960
And so you're gonna see
this very strong prediction

00:38:37.960 --> 00:38:40.010
of the housing characteristics

00:38:40.860 --> 00:38:44.330
based on whether refugees by 2016,

00:38:44.330 --> 00:38:47.590
are living in or outside of camps.

00:38:47.590 --> 00:38:49.620
And so I think that makes a lot of sense

00:38:49.620 --> 00:38:51.980
when we think about kind of
the, the housing characteristics

00:38:51.980 --> 00:38:53.740
and I'll go through that more

00:38:53.740 --> 00:38:56.610
but then there also would
have been kind of other things

00:38:56.610 --> 00:38:59.010
that I would have expected
around household size,

00:38:59.010 --> 00:39:02.540
the number of dependents,
that if this was...

00:39:04.141 --> 00:39:05.520
it could have fed into this story

00:39:05.520 --> 00:39:08.530
and doesn't as much as
I would have expected.

00:39:08.530 --> 00:39:12.480
Also refugees in camps, so
that could have suggested

00:39:12.480 --> 00:39:15.150
this is just a straight
story of negative selection,

00:39:15.150 --> 00:39:18.480
the people who would have
had lower potential earnings

00:39:18.480 --> 00:39:21.740
in the urban labor markets,
are the ones who opt into camps

00:39:21.740 --> 00:39:24.580
and so a lot of this
difference is explained

00:39:24.580 --> 00:39:27.310
just by these kinds of
ex-ante characteristics

00:39:27.310 --> 00:39:29.820
but there's other pieces
of evidence in the data

00:39:29.820 --> 00:39:31.620
that suggests that
that's not the only thing

00:39:31.620 --> 00:39:32.460
that's going on here.

00:39:32.460 --> 00:39:35.330
So refugees just on average,

00:39:35.330 --> 00:39:36.950
were more likely to have vehicles,

00:39:36.950 --> 00:39:38.880
we're more likely to have televisions,

00:39:38.880 --> 00:39:42.770
who ended up living in a camp by 2016.

00:39:42.770 --> 00:39:45.423
They were equally likely to kind of say

00:39:45.423 --> 00:39:47.630
that they meet their basic needs

00:39:47.630 --> 00:39:51.450
and the two most predictive
elements in the data

00:39:51.450 --> 00:39:53.230
of whether you live in a camp or not,

00:39:53.230 --> 00:39:55.970
are just how long you've lived in Jordan,

00:39:55.970 --> 00:39:57.980
so urban residents have lived in Jordan

00:39:57.980 --> 00:40:01.330
for longer than camp residents
and whether you lived closer

00:40:01.330 --> 00:40:05.403
to the border of Jordan
before displacement.

00:40:07.900 --> 00:40:10.650
So as I said, it's gonna, I think a lot

00:40:10.650 --> 00:40:12.930
of what's going on here is
this just willingness to live

00:40:12.930 --> 00:40:16.310
in one of these caravans
that they were distributed

00:40:17.450 --> 00:40:19.680
and it was kind of part of
the built up environment

00:40:19.680 --> 00:40:21.037
in these camps.

00:40:21.037 --> 00:40:23.710
And so here's a sense
of what these look like,

00:40:23.710 --> 00:40:27.260
they're basically like these,
these shipping containers

00:40:28.600 --> 00:40:31.250
that refugees were, were given.

00:40:31.250 --> 00:40:35.770
And so they started out in these
canvas tents until mid 2013

00:40:35.770 --> 00:40:39.570
and then moved to these, these caravans

00:40:39.570 --> 00:40:42.490
and I think that your
just willingness to live

00:40:42.490 --> 00:40:45.120
in these caravans, was what's
driving a lot of this sorting.

00:40:45.120 --> 00:40:48.070
So I'm gonna have some evidence

00:40:48.070 --> 00:40:50.860
that the more space per person,
kind of you were allocated

00:40:50.860 --> 00:40:53.100
upon your arrival to camps,

00:40:53.100 --> 00:40:55.800
the more likely you were to stay

00:40:55.800 --> 00:40:58.520
and that's gonna be just one
of the main driving factors

00:40:58.520 --> 00:41:02.290
much more than your kinda
potential labor market outcomes

00:41:02.290 --> 00:41:05.950
or correlating with your
2010 labor market outcomes,

00:41:05.950 --> 00:41:08.150
that's really explaining
a lot of the story.

00:41:10.590 --> 00:41:12.560
So going along that, we look

00:41:12.560 --> 00:41:14.740
at this, again, these random samples

00:41:14.740 --> 00:41:18.407
of the males and females on
their labor market outcomes

00:41:18.407 --> 00:41:20.270
and we don't see a significant difference

00:41:20.270 --> 00:41:24.820
in terms of education, it's
almost exactly the same

00:41:24.820 --> 00:41:27.850
in terms of whether they
were in school in 2010,

00:41:27.850 --> 00:41:30.260
we actually see it's not
statistically significant

00:41:30.260 --> 00:41:32.900
for males but it is for females,

00:41:32.900 --> 00:41:36.420
that males and females were
more likely to be employed

00:41:36.420 --> 00:41:41.370
of those who've moved and
who were in camps by 2016

00:41:41.370 --> 00:41:43.940
and consistent with some more

00:41:43.940 --> 00:41:46.110
kind of, on an individual level,

00:41:46.110 --> 00:41:48.073
some kind of positive selection,

00:41:49.410 --> 00:41:54.000
refugees males who had jobs
in 2010, camp residents

00:41:54.000 --> 00:41:57.320
were more likely to have a higher ISCOs

00:41:57.320 --> 00:41:59.100
for a kind of one to four level

00:41:59.100 --> 00:42:03.400
of whether, what skill
level their job require.

00:42:03.400 --> 00:42:06.880
And so overall, this
is not explaining a lot

00:42:06.880 --> 00:42:11.880
of the variation in where
refugees are ending up by 2016

00:42:11.950 --> 00:42:13.950
but it butts up against the story

00:42:13.950 --> 00:42:16.710
that this is just, these are the people

00:42:16.710 --> 00:42:19.770
who would have been, just attain less

00:42:19.770 --> 00:42:22.260
in the labor market overall.

00:42:22.260 --> 00:42:24.430
And so I think this is really a story

00:42:24.430 --> 00:42:25.970
about kind of housing preferences

00:42:25.970 --> 00:42:29.430
and what housing you were
kind of expecting and used to,

00:42:29.430 --> 00:42:31.190
much more than it is necessarily

00:42:31.190 --> 00:42:33.423
about your potential
labor market outcomes.

00:42:35.940 --> 00:42:38.290
That's also supported
when we just ask refugees

00:42:38.290 --> 00:42:41.410
why they were moving

00:42:41.410 --> 00:42:46.150
and so out of the refugees
who located from camp

00:42:46.150 --> 00:42:48.840
to urban areas, the
biggest one is insecurity

00:42:48.840 --> 00:42:51.930
because at the beginning
of Za'atari being open,

00:42:51.930 --> 00:42:54.380
there's no doubt that
there was a massive problem

00:42:55.401 --> 00:42:59.680
with insecurity and those major problems

00:42:59.680 --> 00:43:03.533
were eventually kind of
gotten under control by UNHCR.

00:43:04.580 --> 00:43:06.930
So that's the number one reason

00:43:06.930 --> 00:43:08.760
that the refugees reported leaving,

00:43:08.760 --> 00:43:12.330
the second one is looking
for a bigger or better home.

00:43:12.330 --> 00:43:15.310
So I think this is important

00:43:15.310 --> 00:43:19.290
and kind of goes with the
other empirical data presented,

00:43:19.290 --> 00:43:23.090
the, when we're thinking
about whether this is driven

00:43:23.090 --> 00:43:25.940
by labor market outcomes,
eight people said

00:43:25.940 --> 00:43:28.490
this was work-related
but that's much smaller

00:43:28.490 --> 00:43:30.423
than for instance I was expecting.

00:43:31.280 --> 00:43:33.430
We similarly, when we look
at people who've moved

00:43:33.430 --> 00:43:37.830
from urban to camps, it's a
smaller number but the largest

00:43:37.830 --> 00:43:41.480
besides insecurity in the
urban areas, the largest number

00:43:41.480 --> 00:43:44.650
that people report is
that rent was too high

00:43:44.650 --> 00:43:46.160
again, kind of consistent with housing

00:43:46.160 --> 00:43:48.137
being a major factor for (murmurs).

00:43:51.940 --> 00:43:55.590
So now I'm gonna move into,
I'm gonna take these estimates,

00:43:55.590 --> 00:43:57.730
this kind of story of selection

00:43:57.730 --> 00:44:01.670
and these baseline characteristics

00:44:01.670 --> 00:44:06.610
and I'm going to put these into
a Generalized Random Forest,

00:44:06.610 --> 00:44:11.610
which is an algorithm from
Susan Athey et al in 2018

00:44:11.940 --> 00:44:16.940
that generates a propensity
score around the predictions

00:44:17.270 --> 00:44:22.270
of who's more likely to live
in a camp versus urban area

00:44:23.350 --> 00:44:26.193
within Jordan at the time of the survey.

00:44:27.260 --> 00:44:31.320
And so this algorithm is
going to flexibly capture

00:44:31.320 --> 00:44:34.360
different interactions and
you kinda have higher orders

00:44:34.360 --> 00:44:38.270
of the potential
explanatory variables here

00:44:38.270 --> 00:44:41.310
of who's locating in which area

00:44:41.310 --> 00:44:43.870
and once you kind of tune this

00:44:43.870 --> 00:44:46.850
and take into account all of
these different variables,

00:44:46.850 --> 00:44:51.200
it's with 81% accuracy able to predict

00:44:52.540 --> 00:44:55.670
who's, where refugees
are going to be living

00:44:55.670 --> 00:44:57.747
at the time of, of the survey.

00:44:57.747 --> 00:45:01.380
And so I think this, this
does a fairly good job

00:45:01.380 --> 00:45:04.920
of capturing the, the selection story here

00:45:04.920 --> 00:45:07.940
and the, again, the main
factors that fall out

00:45:07.940 --> 00:45:11.730
are that the arrival month is
the most predictive element

00:45:11.730 --> 00:45:13.530
of where refugees are going to live,

00:45:13.530 --> 00:45:15.840
followed by their housing characteristics

00:45:15.840 --> 00:45:17.503
and their origin,

00:45:19.180 --> 00:45:22.383
origin governorates for
where they end up living.

00:45:23.920 --> 00:45:26.720
Then I'm gonna take this propensity score

00:45:26.720 --> 00:45:30.270
and I'm gonna use this to balance

00:45:30.270 --> 00:45:34.820
across 2010 characteristics,
so I'm gonna use

00:45:34.820 --> 00:45:37.410
the Imbens &amp; Rubin method

00:45:37.410 --> 00:45:41.410
to instead of matching kind
of one for one directly,

00:45:41.410 --> 00:45:45.130
refugees in and out of
camps, I'm going to stratify

00:45:45.130 --> 00:45:47.570
and create different blocks of refugees

00:45:47.570 --> 00:45:51.660
that are mostly similar
within that propensity score.

00:45:51.660 --> 00:45:53.770
And then I'm just gonna
follow the algorithm

00:45:53.770 --> 00:45:56.160
that Imbens and Rubin have, have laid out

00:45:56.160 --> 00:46:00.730
in order to create this,
this blocking estimate

00:46:00.730 --> 00:46:05.730
that gives me this Treatment
on the Treated estimate

00:46:05.980 --> 00:46:09.480
of how much the, to estimate this effect

00:46:09.480 --> 00:46:14.020
of how refugees would
have done outside of camps

00:46:14.020 --> 00:46:15.620
and then I'm gonna present the results

00:46:15.620 --> 00:46:19.340
from Ordinary Least Squares
and from other methods

00:46:19.340 --> 00:46:23.350
in order to just look at
the straight selection

00:46:23.350 --> 00:46:25.173
with just a linear specification.

00:46:26.050 --> 00:46:27.680
And whenever I do that, I'm gonna control

00:46:27.680 --> 00:46:32.320
for the head of household's
age, age squared, the gender,

00:46:32.320 --> 00:46:36.850
their 2010 education, the
months that they were displaced,

00:46:36.850 --> 00:46:38.990
fixed effects for the origin governorate

00:46:38.990 --> 00:46:41.750
and then the linearized propensity score

00:46:41.750 --> 00:46:43.470
that I estimate from this

00:46:43.470 --> 00:46:46.840
in order to capture just those
selection characteristics.

00:46:46.840 --> 00:46:49.940
And then I'm gonna present
two different estimates,

00:46:49.940 --> 00:46:54.020
so one is with, without
governorate fixed effects

00:46:54.020 --> 00:46:57.560
and this is going to then
basically estimate the effect

00:46:57.560 --> 00:47:02.560
of living in a camp,
relative to the average

00:47:02.650 --> 00:47:05.250
of all potential destinations
outside of the camp

00:47:05.250 --> 00:47:07.260
and this is specifically in this case,

00:47:07.260 --> 00:47:09.870
gonna take into account Amman

00:47:09.870 --> 00:47:11.760
where refugees could have gone out,

00:47:11.760 --> 00:47:14.600
gone, ended up had they left the camp

00:47:14.600 --> 00:47:17.660
and then I'm gonna present
another specification

00:47:17.660 --> 00:47:21.850
that estimates within governorate,

00:47:21.850 --> 00:47:24.720
so just comparing essentially refugees

00:47:24.720 --> 00:47:27.560
who were living in Mafraq

00:47:27.560 --> 00:47:30.969
with refugees who lived
in Camp Za'atari next door

00:47:30.969 --> 00:47:33.903
and refugees who lived in Zarqa, relative

00:47:33.903 --> 00:47:34.736
to

00:47:36.904 --> 00:47:40.550
the camps, who lived, the
refugees who live in Azraq

00:47:40.550 --> 00:47:42.000
and the Za'atari Governorate.

00:47:45.290 --> 00:47:47.500
So now I'm gonna get onto the results

00:47:49.600 --> 00:47:51.150
unless there are any questions.

00:47:53.040 --> 00:47:55.320
- Yeah, Thomas, I did have
a couple of quick questions,

00:47:55.320 --> 00:47:57.910
could you go maybe back two slides?

00:47:57.910 --> 00:48:00.270
- Yep, this slide?

00:48:00.270 --> 00:48:02.280
- Yeah, perfect, thanks.

00:48:02.280 --> 00:48:07.000
One question is, how are
you thinking about origin

00:48:07.840 --> 00:48:08.870
as being a predictor?

00:48:08.870 --> 00:48:11.590
Is that, are you thinking maybe
they have existing networks

00:48:11.590 --> 00:48:13.550
in Jordan or something like that?

00:48:13.550 --> 00:48:15.140
- Yes, exactly.

00:48:15.140 --> 00:48:17.357
So almost half of the refugees in Jordan

00:48:17.357 --> 00:48:19.250
are from the Dara'a Governorate,

00:48:19.250 --> 00:48:22.100
which is the Southern
most governorate in Syria

00:48:22.100 --> 00:48:23.530
that borders Jordan

00:48:23.530 --> 00:48:27.320
and so the further that
you came to Jordan,

00:48:27.320 --> 00:48:29.660
the less likely you are to live in a camp

00:48:29.660 --> 00:48:33.090
and I think those people
probably had other people

00:48:33.090 --> 00:48:36.550
that they were, were looking
to meet in, in Jordan already

00:48:36.550 --> 00:48:38.150
and things like that.

00:48:38.150 --> 00:48:42.320
- Got it and then the other
thing I, that maybe put me off

00:48:42.320 --> 00:48:45.010
if you like, could you just
talk me through how to think

00:48:45.010 --> 00:48:50.010
about the combination of the
propensity score and the OLS?

00:48:51.928 --> 00:48:53.360
Or like could you, I'm thinking

00:48:53.360 --> 00:48:57.180
of kind of typical
propensity score measures,

00:48:57.180 --> 00:48:59.260
you would just, you would
do some kind of matching

00:48:59.260 --> 00:49:00.540
based on the propensity score

00:49:00.540 --> 00:49:02.950
and then here you're throwing
it, the propensity score

00:49:02.950 --> 00:49:06.810
into an OLS regression
with some other covariates

00:49:06.810 --> 00:49:09.090
that maybe were already a
part of the propensity score,

00:49:09.090 --> 00:49:11.260
how should I be interpreting
that or thinking about it?

00:49:11.260 --> 00:49:13.670
- Yeah, exactly, great question.

00:49:13.670 --> 00:49:14.503
So

00:49:16.222 --> 00:49:17.940
the algorithm recommended
by Imbens and Rubin

00:49:17.940 --> 00:49:19.300
is to create these blocks,

00:49:19.300 --> 00:49:22.110
so basically I'm gonna
have like six blocks

00:49:22.110 --> 00:49:24.730
that within each one of those blocks,

00:49:24.730 --> 00:49:27.600
I'm going to then run the OLS

00:49:27.600 --> 00:49:30.690
that controls for these
kind of main characteristics

00:49:30.690 --> 00:49:35.690
that I chose ad hoc, in order
to, to net out those effects

00:49:36.420 --> 00:49:38.010
and then that creates a treatment effect

00:49:38.010 --> 00:49:41.810
within each one of these blocks
and then you weight blocks

00:49:41.810 --> 00:49:44.010
by the the number within the blocks

00:49:44.010 --> 00:49:45.810
and by the, those estimates.

00:49:45.810 --> 00:49:48.110
And then I'm just gonna have to display

00:49:48.110 --> 00:49:50.630
just OLS specification
with everybody as well.

00:49:50.630 --> 00:49:51.950
So there's two different things,

00:49:51.950 --> 00:49:56.950
one is kinda within the OLS
and within the stratification

00:49:57.200 --> 00:49:59.150
and then another one is just OLS

00:49:59.150 --> 00:50:01.870
with the linearized propensity score

00:50:01.870 --> 00:50:05.943
and the other explanatory variates.

00:50:07.590 --> 00:50:08.593
- Okay, thanks.

00:50:10.000 --> 00:50:10.833
- Great.

00:50:12.470 --> 00:50:14.430
So the first outcome
that I'm gonna look at,

00:50:14.430 --> 00:50:17.730
is that camps reduce household earning

00:50:17.730 --> 00:50:19.790
and this outcome is gonna
be a total of the wage

00:50:19.790 --> 00:50:21.200
and self-employment income

00:50:22.060 --> 00:50:25.180
that was 30 days prior to
the time of the survey.

00:50:25.180 --> 00:50:29.380
And so I'm gonna look at this
in just the absolute measure

00:50:29.380 --> 00:50:31.770
in terms of USD on the left here

00:50:31.770 --> 00:50:34.800
and then take the inverse hyperbolic sign

00:50:34.800 --> 00:50:37.460
in order to account for these many zeros.

00:50:37.460 --> 00:50:41.640
Again, 60% of households
don't have any earnings

00:50:41.640 --> 00:50:45.357
from this wage and self-employment
income and, in camps

00:50:45.357 --> 00:50:48.467
and 40% don't have any
earnings in urban areas

00:50:48.467 --> 00:50:51.610
and so it's important that
if we just took the log

00:50:51.610 --> 00:50:56.610
we wouldn't be able to include
these households with zero.

00:50:57.690 --> 00:50:59.350
And so this is gonna give me

00:51:00.190 --> 00:51:03.800
these overall household level effects

00:51:04.720 --> 00:51:08.160
and so you can see with, without
governorate fixed effects

00:51:08.160 --> 00:51:12.467
are compared to everybody,
all the urban residents,

00:51:13.640 --> 00:51:18.000
I'm gonna estimate an
effect, I think of $67 here,

00:51:18.000 --> 00:51:20.280
that's statistically significant,

00:51:20.280 --> 00:51:23.210
when I moved to just
estimating to refugees

00:51:23.210 --> 00:51:24.790
within the nearest governorates,

00:51:24.790 --> 00:51:27.720
it's still gonna be a negative
effect for camps on earnings

00:51:27.720 --> 00:51:30.760
but it's actually not
statistically significant.

00:51:30.760 --> 00:51:32.320
And I find basically the same thing,

00:51:32.320 --> 00:51:36.303
when I transform the inverse of log Sin.

00:51:39.400 --> 00:51:41.680
This is that same thing
in regression form,

00:51:41.680 --> 00:51:44.110
so that's the, from these
blocks again, I've got

00:51:44.110 --> 00:51:47.450
kind of six blocks here
that I put all together

00:51:47.450 --> 00:51:52.450
that gives me this $67 effect
off of an urban mean of $192

00:51:55.770 --> 00:51:58.600
and so that's the kind
of first specification,

00:51:58.600 --> 00:52:01.470
it's very similar when I don't worry

00:52:01.470 --> 00:52:05.130
about this blocking estimate
and just pull everything

00:52:05.130 --> 00:52:06.690
into the same regression

00:52:07.900 --> 00:52:11.010
and this is gonna give me $63

00:52:12.690 --> 00:52:17.560
wage penalty for living in a
camp and then the Oster Delta

00:52:17.560 --> 00:52:20.750
for whether the unobservables
could potentially overturn

00:52:20.750 --> 00:52:24.410
these results is above one,
which means that we wouldn't,

00:52:24.410 --> 00:52:27.293
would expect that selection
is doing on observables

00:52:27.293 --> 00:52:29.150
it's doing a fairly good job here

00:52:29.150 --> 00:52:32.573
and the unobservables
wouldn't overturn this result.

00:52:34.520 --> 00:52:36.430
So these results are driven largely

00:52:36.430 --> 00:52:38.730
by lower male employment,

00:52:38.730 --> 00:52:43.000
45% male employment in urban regions

00:52:43.000 --> 00:52:48.000
relative to 32% for camp residents

00:52:48.320 --> 00:52:53.320
and so that's where the
majority of the earnings penalty

00:52:53.700 --> 00:52:56.903
is coming from for living
in a camp in this setting.

00:52:58.240 --> 00:52:59.729
I was also surprised to see

00:52:59.729 --> 00:53:02.750
there's actually a positive
effect for females,

00:53:02.750 --> 00:53:04.170
for female employment.

00:53:04.170 --> 00:53:09.170
So it is, in this case,
there's 9% employment for women

00:53:10.730 --> 00:53:13.477
in camps versus 2% outside of camps

00:53:13.477 --> 00:53:15.690
and I think in most of
what's going on here,

00:53:15.690 --> 00:53:20.510
86% of the employment for women in camps

00:53:20.510 --> 00:53:24.640
is through non-profits
basically looking to have

00:53:24.640 --> 00:53:27.100
kind of equitable access to these jobs

00:53:27.100 --> 00:53:29.790
and so that's a substantial
and I think important story

00:53:29.790 --> 00:53:31.540
but it's not enough to kinda compensate

00:53:31.540 --> 00:53:33.343
for the overall household earnings.

00:53:36.350 --> 00:53:39.650
So then I'm gonna move into
calculating this net income

00:53:40.520 --> 00:53:43.610
per capita and that's
gonna take these earnings,

00:53:43.610 --> 00:53:48.120
so from self-employment,
from, from wage employment

00:53:48.120 --> 00:53:49.720
and I'm gonna add the other income,

00:53:49.720 --> 00:53:52.710
I'm gonna add assistance,
then I'm gonna subtract out

00:53:52.710 --> 00:53:56.010
a conservative estimate
of the minimum expenses

00:53:56.010 --> 00:54:00.260
that refugees might have
faced outside of the camp.

00:54:00.260 --> 00:54:02.820
And so in order to calculate that,

00:54:02.820 --> 00:54:06.270
I'm gonna take the 10th percentile of rent

00:54:06.270 --> 00:54:07.380
within the governorates

00:54:07.380 --> 00:54:10.040
that refugees outside of camps would pay.

00:54:10.040 --> 00:54:15.040
So I'm gonna standardize the
expenses in camps to zero

00:54:15.470 --> 00:54:19.930
and then subtract out the minimum expenses

00:54:19.930 --> 00:54:23.350
that refugees are paying in urban areas

00:54:23.350 --> 00:54:26.980
but take a very conservative
estimate of that.

00:54:26.980 --> 00:54:29.690
They're also paying more
outside of camps for school

00:54:29.690 --> 00:54:31.260
and for health costs

00:54:31.260 --> 00:54:33.480
and I'm not even going to
factor those into account,

00:54:33.480 --> 00:54:36.220
again, to give kind of a
very conservative estimate

00:54:36.220 --> 00:54:39.853
of how much is being saved
from living in a camp.

00:54:41.040 --> 00:54:43.760
Another kind of outcome
we would think about

00:54:43.760 --> 00:54:46.210
or to, in order to benchmark
the expense food prices

00:54:46.210 --> 00:54:49.633
but the median food prices
reported inside and outside camps

00:54:49.633 --> 00:54:52.390
are identical in this setting.

00:54:52.390 --> 00:54:55.270
And then I'm gonna convert
this to per capita estimates

00:54:55.270 --> 00:54:58.270
in order to standardize with
the cost estimates that I have.

00:55:00.130 --> 00:55:01.890
So when I just look at the total income

00:55:01.890 --> 00:55:05.440
per capita here first, I still
get this negative effect,

00:55:05.440 --> 00:55:10.440
about $10 per person per
month of living in a camp

00:55:11.630 --> 00:55:16.630
but when I subtract out these
net with minimum expenses,

00:55:17.030 --> 00:55:19.850
this is when I get kind of
the overall positive effect

00:55:19.850 --> 00:55:22.740
of net expenses of living in a camp

00:55:22.740 --> 00:55:25.220
and because there's kind
of a different expense

00:55:26.130 --> 00:55:28.820
subtracted out for different governorates,

00:55:28.820 --> 00:55:31.800
I'm gonna get a similar
coefficient, a similar effect

00:55:31.800 --> 00:55:33.950
whether you compare them to everybody

00:55:33.950 --> 00:55:37.270
including those living in
Amman or to the refugees

00:55:37.270 --> 00:55:39.510
who are living in the
most kind of similar areas

00:55:39.510 --> 00:55:44.510
of about $11 per person per
month net for camp residents.

00:55:49.110 --> 00:55:50.920
Some additional evidence that I have here

00:55:50.920 --> 00:55:51.753
is that

00:55:53.520 --> 00:55:57.950
in order to, so refugees inside of camps

00:55:57.950 --> 00:56:00.677
are much less in debt than
refugees outside of camps

00:56:00.677 --> 00:56:02.890
and the story that I
think explains this best

00:56:02.890 --> 00:56:05.110
is that refugees outside of camps

00:56:05.110 --> 00:56:08.250
are having to go into more debt

00:56:08.250 --> 00:56:11.780
in order to afford their
larger housing costs.

00:56:11.780 --> 00:56:15.140
So these estimates are very large,

00:56:15.140 --> 00:56:20.110
about 80% of refugees
in the urban settings,

00:56:20.110 --> 00:56:23.350
who expressed a debt
of at least 100 dinar,

00:56:23.350 --> 00:56:28.350
which is $140 and that's
20 percentage points less

00:56:28.870 --> 00:56:33.870
in the urban area, in for
refugees who are living in camps.

00:56:35.700 --> 00:56:37.860
With, I'm also not seeing any difference

00:56:37.860 --> 00:56:39.030
in access to credit

00:56:39.905 --> 00:56:42.060
and so that, that wouldn't
explain this either,

00:56:42.060 --> 00:56:45.410
I'm, then if you asked just
in this subjective measure

00:56:45.410 --> 00:56:48.330
of whether refugees consider
their household to be poor

00:56:48.330 --> 00:56:51.910
or not, it's actually lower

00:56:51.910 --> 00:56:56.910
that fewer refugees consider
themselves poor in camps

00:56:56.930 --> 00:56:59.350
than outside of campus, isn't
statistically significant

00:56:59.350 --> 00:57:01.520
but I think also kind of illustrative

00:57:01.520 --> 00:57:06.210
that the financial situation
for refugees outside of camps

00:57:06.210 --> 00:57:08.710
may be much worse, 84% here

00:57:09.670 --> 00:57:12.300
than I think we kind of have in mind

00:57:12.300 --> 00:57:14.923
when we wanna think about
urban refugee situations.

00:57:17.950 --> 00:57:21.360
So I should note also here
that there's fewer assets

00:57:21.360 --> 00:57:25.580
for refugees who are in, in
camps in terms of TVs, radios

00:57:25.580 --> 00:57:28.000
and appliances, I think this has changed,

00:57:28.000 --> 00:57:29.887
this is increasing over time

00:57:29.887 --> 00:57:32.140
and so converging in some sense

00:57:32.140 --> 00:57:35.840
but it's not that, it's
an important difference

00:57:35.840 --> 00:57:38.490
that's also should be
noted, when we're thinking

00:57:38.490 --> 00:57:40.790
about kind of these
total, total effects here.

00:57:43.090 --> 00:57:48.090
So then a major concern about
refugees over the long-run

00:57:49.760 --> 00:57:51.110
and living in camps

00:57:51.110 --> 00:57:55.680
is how these effects are going to evolve.

00:57:55.680 --> 00:57:59.170
And so what I do for this,
again refugees on average

00:57:59.170 --> 00:58:02.000
have only been in these
camps about 2 1/2 years

00:58:03.920 --> 00:58:06.840
but I'm gonna look at those
refugees who were in the camps

00:58:06.840 --> 00:58:09.480
when we asked about September, 2013

00:58:10.410 --> 00:58:12.350
and I'm going to look at
their employment outcomes

00:58:12.350 --> 00:58:14.900
at that point relative to their outcomes

00:58:14.900 --> 00:58:17.870
when we surveyed them
in the spring of 2016.

00:58:17.870 --> 00:58:20.360
And so if you kind of think
about the parallel trends

00:58:20.360 --> 00:58:21.890
that if you assume that
they would have been

00:58:21.890 --> 00:58:25.350
on parallel trends if
not for living on camps,

00:58:25.350 --> 00:58:26.700
then you can interpret this

00:58:28.584 --> 00:58:29.580
as kind of

00:58:29.580 --> 00:58:31.353
the difference of camps.

00:58:32.240 --> 00:58:35.380
And so you'll see here that
while the employment is lower

00:58:35.380 --> 00:58:39.803
for males in camps,
they're on a similar trend,

00:58:41.610 --> 00:58:43.730
increase over this time period,

00:58:43.730 --> 00:58:45.510
wage growth is a little bit higher

00:58:45.510 --> 00:58:49.460
for refugees outside of camps

00:58:49.460 --> 00:58:53.260
but growing also for
refugees inside of camps.

00:58:53.260 --> 00:58:56.450
And then I've also gone
back to the 2018 survey,

00:58:56.450 --> 00:58:57.860
so two years later

00:58:57.860 --> 00:59:00.270
and this employment
has basically converged

00:59:00.270 --> 00:59:03.380
between refugees outside of
camps and inside of camps.

00:59:03.380 --> 00:59:07.000
So 46% employment outside of camps

00:59:07.000 --> 00:59:10.590
but up to 44% inside of camps

00:59:10.590 --> 00:59:13.040
and so it seems like the refugees

00:59:13.040 --> 00:59:17.960
are actually on even a faster
employment growth at least,

00:59:17.960 --> 00:59:19.963
within camps by 2018.

00:59:21.940 --> 00:59:24.150
When we look at women,
it's important to note

00:59:24.150 --> 00:59:27.690
that they started off
higher, employment percentage

00:59:27.690 --> 00:59:30.520
and so there might be some
kind of selection bias

00:59:30.520 --> 00:59:31.353
going on here

00:59:32.260 --> 00:59:36.000
but this is also a substantially
larger wage growth,

00:59:36.000 --> 00:59:38.570
again, I think mostly
attributed to non-profits

00:59:40.050 --> 00:59:42.450
but it also persists into 2018,

00:59:42.450 --> 00:59:44.930
there's 8%, percentage points

00:59:44.930 --> 00:59:48.470
versus three percentage points by 2018.

00:59:48.470 --> 00:59:50.120
Just not enough really to estimate

00:59:50.120 --> 00:59:53.950
kind of wage, wage effects
here and then the differences

00:59:55.090 --> 00:59:57.950
but I think this, again, it
doesn't provide evidence,

00:59:57.950 --> 01:00:01.330
much evidence that refugees
in camps are stagnating

01:00:01.330 --> 01:00:04.480
much more than refugees outside of camps,

01:00:04.480 --> 01:00:07.810
that is just an important
thing to kind of keep in mind

01:00:07.810 --> 01:00:12.300
and to continue to survey these
populations and to monitor

01:00:12.300 --> 01:00:15.563
as we think about the long-run aspects.

01:00:18.450 --> 01:00:20.900
Then we wanna think about
amenities and services,

01:00:21.780 --> 01:00:25.030
so I'm gonna find actually the
primary school aged children

01:00:25.030 --> 01:00:29.437
in camps are more likely to
be in school by two, in 2016

01:00:30.790 --> 01:00:33.440
and so it's not, it's not a lot

01:00:34.960 --> 01:00:37.290
but here at very at much younger ages,

01:00:37.290 --> 01:00:41.850
we see that they are more
likely to be in, in school

01:00:41.850 --> 01:00:45.620
and this drops off for both
camp and urban refugees

01:00:45.620 --> 01:00:48.900
as we get into secondary
schooling, unfortunately here

01:00:48.900 --> 01:00:53.160
but not substantial evidence
that children in camp schools

01:00:53.160 --> 01:00:55.913
are at least attending much less.

01:00:56.960 --> 01:00:58.410
- Does school attendance differ,

01:00:58.410 --> 01:01:02.150
sorry to interrupt, does school
attendance differ by gender?

01:01:02.150 --> 01:01:04.853
- No, not that I, not that
I was picking up here.

01:01:05.910 --> 01:01:07.570
- And do you have a good explanation

01:01:07.570 --> 01:01:11.300
for why attendance for
five years old is so low

01:01:11.300 --> 01:01:12.333
in urban areas?

01:01:14.120 --> 01:01:15.700
- I don't have much of a sense,

01:01:15.700 --> 01:01:19.530
I think that one guess could
be that urban households

01:01:19.530 --> 01:01:21.140
were waiting a little bit to,

01:01:21.140 --> 01:01:23.240
because there are some
school fees associated

01:01:23.240 --> 01:01:26.170
in urban areas, whether
those are formal or informal

01:01:26.170 --> 01:01:29.210
but they're just like
delaying a little bit,

01:01:29.210 --> 01:01:30.370
would be one explanation

01:01:30.370 --> 01:01:33.180
and also like efforts within the camp

01:01:33.180 --> 01:01:36.043
in order to get more kind of
school, children in school.

01:01:36.960 --> 01:01:40.220
- Maybe because there are
not enough preschools as well

01:01:40.220 --> 01:01:44.360
or the age, the legal
age for entering school

01:01:44.360 --> 01:01:48.870
is set around six probably
or something like that.

01:01:48.870 --> 01:01:53.410
- Yeah, I don't have, I don't
have a great, great sense

01:01:53.410 --> 01:01:56.040
but it at least doesn't suggest

01:01:56.040 --> 01:01:59.090
that they're doing much worse
in camps than outside of camp.

01:01:59.090 --> 01:02:02.270
- Sorry, Thomas, I have one
more question about the schools,

01:02:02.270 --> 01:02:03.660
I just wanna compare them to Lebanon,

01:02:03.660 --> 01:02:06.230
are they mixed shifts or one shift,

01:02:06.230 --> 01:02:08.320
combining Jordanians and Syrians together

01:02:08.320 --> 01:02:11.030
because this is an issue that
we're facing here in Lebanon.

01:02:11.030 --> 01:02:12.920
- Yes, so just...

01:02:12.920 --> 01:02:15.000
- Sorry if I, I didn't finish yet,

01:02:15.000 --> 01:02:18.160
plus the curriculum actually,
that was actually an issue.

01:02:18.160 --> 01:02:21.230
This is why the Syrian
curriculum in teaching,

01:02:21.230 --> 01:02:23.820
hoping for the kids to return back home,

01:02:23.820 --> 01:02:25.540
is different than the Lebanese one,

01:02:25.540 --> 01:02:27.780
so there was also another issue,
I just, I'm just wondering

01:02:27.780 --> 01:02:30.090
if this is the same case in Jordan

01:02:30.090 --> 01:02:33.230
- Yes, so Jordan also
opened the two shifts

01:02:33.230 --> 01:02:35.650
in high prevalence areas,

01:02:35.650 --> 01:02:38.820
so I actually have another
program, another project

01:02:38.820 --> 01:02:43.450
that demonstrates how this,
basically Jordanian students

01:02:43.450 --> 01:02:46.570
were not very exposed to Syrian students

01:02:46.570 --> 01:02:48.560
but the prevalence of
Syrians in these schools

01:02:48.560 --> 01:02:51.150
was pretty low and Syrians were mostly

01:02:51.150 --> 01:02:55.540
in this kind of second
shift of, of education.

01:02:55.540 --> 01:02:59.640
So it's very similar in the
Jordanian context as well.

01:02:59.640 --> 01:03:01.090
Thanks.

01:03:01.090 --> 01:03:02.393
- Thank you.

01:03:02.393 --> 01:03:04.270
- And so then public services,

01:03:04.270 --> 01:03:06.210
people are less satisfied in camps

01:03:06.210 --> 01:03:09.283
but overall people feel,
report feeling very safe,

01:03:10.140 --> 01:03:15.140
safe or very safe, both
inside and outside camps.

01:03:15.507 --> 01:03:16.880
And just one more point here,

01:03:16.880 --> 01:03:19.730
is that women in camps list
a lot more regular contacts

01:03:19.730 --> 01:03:22.290
than urban women, one thing I noticed

01:03:22.290 --> 01:03:24.700
when I was kind of
doing part of the survey

01:03:24.700 --> 01:03:28.890
was women who were in urban
areas were expressing loneliness

01:03:28.890 --> 01:03:31.910
and isolation from not having
the kind of same networks

01:03:31.910 --> 01:03:36.910
that, and being afraid to,
to leave and be able to find

01:03:36.910 --> 01:03:41.910
kind of, build a community
like refugees in camps had

01:03:42.077 --> 01:03:44.243
and that's also born out in the data.

01:03:45.230 --> 01:03:48.140
- So just a quick clarifying
question, you mentioned earlier

01:03:48.140 --> 01:03:52.050
that concerns about
safety were a major driver

01:03:52.050 --> 01:03:55.370
of choosing living in a
camp versus urban areas

01:03:55.370 --> 01:03:58.320
but then you find that
actually everyone feels safe

01:03:58.320 --> 01:04:00.510
regardless of where they end up living.

01:04:00.510 --> 01:04:02.214
- Yep...
- Is that correct?

01:04:02.214 --> 01:04:05.110
- That's definitely a
function of at the beginning,

01:04:05.110 --> 01:04:09.850
these camps were very insecure
and the government and UNHCR

01:04:09.850 --> 01:04:12.980
has done a lot to, to
improve security over time.

01:04:12.980 --> 01:04:16.150
So that's an evolution over time.

01:04:16.150 --> 01:04:17.630
- Got it, thanks.

01:04:17.630 --> 01:04:19.970
- Sorry, Thomas, can I interrupt..

01:04:19.970 --> 01:04:20.940
Sorry, go ahead.

01:04:20.940 --> 01:04:22.220
- No, go ahead, I'm
gonna, I was gonna move

01:04:22.220 --> 01:04:23.400
to the next thing.

01:04:24.570 --> 01:04:27.330
- Just, yeah, just regarding,
probably we're backpedaling

01:04:27.330 --> 01:04:29.770
a little bit, regarding
the demographic studies

01:04:29.770 --> 01:04:31.590
when you were mentioning
the employment for women

01:04:31.590 --> 01:04:34.050
did you also consider that some women,

01:04:34.050 --> 01:04:35.340
because this is the case I'm facing

01:04:35.340 --> 01:04:37.740
when I'm doing my, when I
finished my infographic studies,

01:04:37.740 --> 01:04:41.810
the results that came from
that, that most of them

01:04:41.810 --> 01:04:45.810
who are employed are
usually either widowed

01:04:45.810 --> 01:04:50.810
or their husband is jailed
or got lost during the war.

01:04:51.160 --> 01:04:54.180
So I'm not sure if you
factored in this variable

01:04:54.180 --> 01:04:57.203
to justify the employment
of women increasing.

01:04:58.490 --> 01:05:01.020
- That's interesting, I
will, I'll look at that,

01:05:01.020 --> 01:05:03.970
I would suspect that there's
some of that going on,

01:05:03.970 --> 01:05:05.700
again, my main explanation here,

01:05:05.700 --> 01:05:10.700
there's very little women
employment in urban areas at all

01:05:12.040 --> 01:05:15.220
and so not really enough
to be able to tease out

01:05:15.220 --> 01:05:17.350
whether it's a male or
female head of household

01:05:17.350 --> 01:05:20.023
but within camps, I can
look at that a little bit,

01:05:21.400 --> 01:05:24.230
we do have their household
demographics from 2010,

01:05:24.230 --> 01:05:28.070
so let me get back to you on
that, again, most of the story

01:05:28.070 --> 01:05:30.820
I think is really the opportunity
provided by nonprofits

01:05:30.820 --> 01:05:34.130
in the camps but that,
still within that employment

01:05:34.130 --> 01:05:36.180
might be the story that you have in mind.

01:05:37.670 --> 01:05:39.350
- Okay, thank you.

01:05:39.350 --> 01:05:41.360
- So again, kind of going to...

01:05:41.360 --> 01:05:42.550
Oh, there a question...
- There's one question,

01:05:42.550 --> 01:05:45.190
yeah, there is a question
there on the "Chat"

01:05:45.190 --> 01:05:49.230
about the, if male-headed
households are more likely

01:05:49.230 --> 01:05:51.823
to be in urban areas than female-headed.

01:05:53.700 --> 01:05:56.580
- Female-headed households
are a little bit more likely

01:05:56.580 --> 01:06:01.170
to be in, in camps than outside of camps

01:06:01.170 --> 01:06:02.443
but not substantially.

01:06:04.639 --> 01:06:06.710
It's not a major predictor

01:06:06.710 --> 01:06:09.310
of who's going to locate in which area.

01:06:09.310 --> 01:06:10.620
Thanks.

01:06:10.620 --> 01:06:12.400
So we are talking about
kind of the evolution

01:06:12.400 --> 01:06:15.200
over time here and this is
the, the Cantril Ladder.

01:06:15.200 --> 01:06:18.070
So asking people to recall
that has all kinds of problems

01:06:18.070 --> 01:06:20.220
but I think it's still useful exercise

01:06:20.220 --> 01:06:24.010
where high here is, "My life
could not be any better"

01:06:24.010 --> 01:06:26.180
and one here's, "My life
could not be any worse"

01:06:26.180 --> 01:06:30.810
and so consistent with there being a...

01:06:30.810 --> 01:06:33.430
the camps improving over time,

01:06:33.430 --> 01:06:35.920
we see on these blue lines
here, these are people

01:06:35.920 --> 01:06:38.583
who were in Za'atari in 2013,

01:06:40.480 --> 01:06:44.000
the conflict started and
satisfaction goes way down,

01:06:44.000 --> 01:06:45.420
many of these stayed in Syria

01:06:45.420 --> 01:06:47.320
for the initial parts of the conflict

01:06:47.320 --> 01:06:51.070
and then the camp is a
really rough place to be

01:06:51.070 --> 01:06:53.300
at this time and we see
kind of steady improvement

01:06:53.300 --> 01:06:54.870
to the point where at
the time of the survey,

01:06:54.870 --> 01:06:59.870
that we see no differences
between Za'atari and urban areas,

01:06:59.940 --> 01:07:03.620
we see a similar kind of
U-shaped trend here in Azraq

01:07:03.620 --> 01:07:06.440
but Azraq is still, people report

01:07:07.290 --> 01:07:10.240
substantially lower satisfaction in Azraq

01:07:10.240 --> 01:07:13.380
and I think that's because it's
more isolated, it's hotter,

01:07:13.380 --> 01:07:15.120
it's just worst place to be

01:07:15.120 --> 01:07:18.060
and I'll talk about that at the end,

01:07:18.060 --> 01:07:21.690
I don't see any differences
on the employment,

01:07:21.690 --> 01:07:24.510
income effects that I've
talked about earlier

01:07:24.510 --> 01:07:27.950
and so I pulled those together
but on the satisfaction,

01:07:27.950 --> 01:07:30.000
differences most definitely emerged here.

01:07:31.500 --> 01:07:33.460
When I moved to Kurdistan here,

01:07:33.460 --> 01:07:35.150
there is a very, very different setting,

01:07:35.150 --> 01:07:37.990
refugees had access to work,
they had freedom of movement

01:07:37.990 --> 01:07:39.920
to leave the camps completely

01:07:39.920 --> 01:07:42.120
and so I think this is just a better model

01:07:42.120 --> 01:07:44.470
from especially like a
human rights perspective

01:07:45.503 --> 01:07:48.713
and just a better model overall,

01:07:48.713 --> 01:07:53.580
I see similar effects in
terms of the potential,

01:07:53.580 --> 01:07:55.190
the camp wage penalties.

01:07:55.190 --> 01:07:59.510
So substantial wage penalty
from living in a camp

01:07:59.510 --> 01:08:02.840
that refugees earn much less

01:08:02.840 --> 01:08:05.210
but when I subtract out
the minimum expenses

01:08:05.210 --> 01:08:07.110
in the same way that I did for Jordan,

01:08:08.010 --> 01:08:10.920
you see this positive effect
here, the blue for Jordan,

01:08:10.920 --> 01:08:12.560
this is basically a zero in camps,

01:08:12.560 --> 01:08:15.870
so this wage penalty is,
is basically netted out

01:08:15.870 --> 01:08:19.937
by the rent that camp refugees save

01:08:21.450 --> 01:08:22.993
in the Kurdistan region.

01:08:25.370 --> 01:08:27.320
So now the last couple of slides here,

01:08:27.320 --> 01:08:29.410
so cost-effectiveness.

01:08:29.410 --> 01:08:34.410
so there's not many estimates
of the cost across locations,

01:08:34.830 --> 01:08:38.500
I'm trying to pull the
two main ones that do this

01:08:38.500 --> 01:08:41.130
in 2014 was the best I could find

01:08:41.130 --> 01:08:45.510
but the nonprofits through the
Syrian Response Plan estimate

01:08:45.510 --> 01:08:48.990
that it's about $60 per person
per month, more expensive

01:08:48.990 --> 01:08:53.720
to serve refugees in camps
than it is outside of camps.

01:08:53.720 --> 01:08:55.960
I'm gonna take this
estimate, then I'm gonna look

01:08:55.960 --> 01:09:00.070
at this USAID study on the
fiscal effects from 2014

01:09:00.070 --> 01:09:02.470
and this is gonna provide the estimates

01:09:02.470 --> 01:09:04.240
from the government expenditures.

01:09:04.240 --> 01:09:05.700
So how much the...

01:09:05.700 --> 01:09:08.090
whatever is going through
the Jordanian government

01:09:08.090 --> 01:09:11.820
in terms of education, health,
electricity, subsidies,

01:09:11.820 --> 01:09:14.800
municipal services, food and gas subsidies

01:09:14.800 --> 01:09:17.090
and this is net of the
Regional Response Plan

01:09:17.090 --> 01:09:19.620
so the gross budget
support is going on here

01:09:19.620 --> 01:09:21.590
and that's for the government,

01:09:21.590 --> 01:09:23.970
it's $51 per person more expensive

01:09:26.719 --> 01:09:29.520
to serve refugees outside of camps.

01:09:29.520 --> 01:09:32.920
So overall that's a $9 difference
plus the $11 difference

01:09:32.920 --> 01:09:33.960
that I messed with in here

01:09:33.960 --> 01:09:36.413
again, it's a very rough
estimate of about $2.

01:09:39.360 --> 01:09:42.060
Now we think about the
general equilibrium.

01:09:42.060 --> 01:09:43.930
So think about the thought experiment

01:09:43.930 --> 01:09:47.410
of that Jordan has never opened camps

01:09:47.410 --> 01:09:49.953
and they host the same number of refugees.

01:09:50.990 --> 01:09:53.700
So this would represent a 22% increase

01:09:53.700 --> 01:09:56.683
in the urban refugee population,

01:09:58.540 --> 01:10:01.260
that then we think about
the effects on refugees

01:10:01.260 --> 01:10:03.070
and on the host communities.

01:10:03.070 --> 01:10:06.040
So for refugees, potentially
there will be crowd out

01:10:06.040 --> 01:10:08.690
of each other from housing and from jobs

01:10:08.690 --> 01:10:10.420
and that would mean that
these current estimates

01:10:10.420 --> 01:10:13.330
actually understate the camp surplus

01:10:13.330 --> 01:10:14.540
that I'm estimating here

01:10:16.480 --> 01:10:19.180
but for Jordanians, like I said earlier,

01:10:19.180 --> 01:10:22.137
the main effects are not from income,

01:10:22.137 --> 01:10:25.460
are not from public services
in terms of education

01:10:25.460 --> 01:10:28.710
or from access to healthcare
that Syrians are crowding

01:10:28.710 --> 01:10:30.810
and Jordanians are having
a negative effect on,

01:10:30.810 --> 01:10:32.010
it's the cost of living.

01:10:32.010 --> 01:10:35.450
And so that there's very
inelastic supply of housing

01:10:35.450 --> 01:10:36.850
and so there's two different papers

01:10:36.850 --> 01:10:40.830
that estimate large increases from Syrians

01:10:40.830 --> 01:10:43.000
who locate close to Jordanians,

01:10:43.000 --> 01:10:45.090
having a substantial negative effect

01:10:46.734 --> 01:10:49.400
on the Jordanians in that area.

01:10:49.400 --> 01:10:52.810
And so if we think about
again, refugee camps

01:10:52.810 --> 01:10:54.400
is providing this additional housing

01:10:54.400 --> 01:10:55.790
would alleviate some of that burden,

01:10:55.790 --> 01:10:58.700
I think that's a very
important kind of a factor

01:10:58.700 --> 01:11:02.900
to build in here when we're
thinking about the overall net

01:11:02.900 --> 01:11:05.787
of camps and what camps that could be.

01:11:05.787 --> 01:11:08.710
The other thing I'll say
quickly is that if camps close,

01:11:08.710 --> 01:11:10.070
you can think about the aid budget

01:11:10.070 --> 01:11:11.200
potentially being affected.

01:11:11.200 --> 01:11:13.330
So one Jordanian official said me

01:11:13.330 --> 01:11:16.480
that the, one of the
arguments for opening a camp,

01:11:16.480 --> 01:11:19.030
was simply because they
learned their lesson

01:11:19.030 --> 01:11:20.030
with Palestinians

01:11:20.030 --> 01:11:23.590
that the aid money they
suspected was much less

01:11:23.590 --> 01:11:25.660
because they allowed people to assimilate

01:11:26.888 --> 01:11:29.260
and so with Syrians, they
wanted to keep them visible

01:11:29.260 --> 01:11:30.900
in order to keep the aid money flowing

01:11:30.900 --> 01:11:32.300
and I'm actually going
to be looking at this

01:11:32.300 --> 01:11:35.263
in another project forthcoming.

01:11:37.130 --> 01:11:39.180
So my final slide here

01:11:39.180 --> 01:11:42.490
is just kind of what, what are
we, what should we take away

01:11:42.490 --> 01:11:45.500
from this estimates, from,
from this discussion?

01:11:45.500 --> 01:11:49.090
And so I think about this
as Za'atari and Kurdistan,

01:11:49.090 --> 01:11:53.330
as being kind of the lower
bounds of what camps,

01:11:53.330 --> 01:11:54.770
and I put that in quotation marks,

01:11:54.770 --> 01:11:58.140
as like suburbs or enclaves could be

01:11:58.140 --> 01:12:00.130
for a humanitarian response.

01:12:00.130 --> 01:12:02.980
So thinking about making
these areas attractive

01:12:02.980 --> 01:12:05.910
for refugees, it could be a win-win

01:12:05.910 --> 01:12:09.670
but that I think this, this
environment shows the design

01:12:09.670 --> 01:12:11.530
and the content are key.

01:12:11.530 --> 01:12:16.530
So that, it's possible that
returns to in-kind shelter

01:12:16.800 --> 01:12:18.810
or even higher than the cash equivalent,

01:12:18.810 --> 01:12:22.880
when we kind of factor
everything in here overall,

01:12:22.880 --> 01:12:25.950
again, owing to the fact
that this housing supply,

01:12:25.950 --> 01:12:28.110
is so inelastic in the short-run

01:12:28.110 --> 01:12:30.740
and builders are gonna find it risky

01:12:30.740 --> 01:12:33.380
to invest in this population
that they don't know exactly

01:12:33.380 --> 01:12:34.750
how long they're going to be staying,

01:12:34.750 --> 01:12:36.180
refugees want to go home,

01:12:36.180 --> 01:12:38.060
host communities want them to go home

01:12:38.060 --> 01:12:40.730
and so the housing market
might not immediately respond

01:12:40.730 --> 01:12:42.680
with like standard housing

01:12:42.680 --> 01:12:45.260
in order to house a refugee population

01:12:45.260 --> 01:12:47.560
and that shelter here is just
gonna to be a key constraint

01:12:47.560 --> 01:12:49.773
for both refugees and for hosts.

01:12:51.220 --> 01:12:52.270
But providing that shelter

01:12:52.270 --> 01:12:54.240
does not have to imply
the other restrictions

01:12:54.240 --> 01:12:55.710
that we often associate with camps.

01:12:55.710 --> 01:12:58.580
These camps don't need to
be isolated in the same ways

01:12:58.580 --> 01:13:00.387
that we see in, in the Jordanian context

01:13:00.387 --> 01:13:03.770
and in other contexts, you
don't need to impose limits

01:13:03.770 --> 01:13:08.540
on movement or restrictions
on, on permanent building,

01:13:08.540 --> 01:13:10.840
those are likely lose-lose situations.

01:13:10.840 --> 01:13:15.010
So we see from the examples
in Azraq relative to Za'atari

01:13:15.010 --> 01:13:18.550
that fewer refugees are going
to stay in more isolated,

01:13:18.550 --> 01:13:22.960
in worse areas, they're gonna
be higher costs for nonprofits

01:13:22.960 --> 01:13:26.720
to engage in the water
trucking and waste management

01:13:26.720 --> 01:13:28.750
in more remote areas.

01:13:28.750 --> 01:13:30.860
And so these are just lose-lose,

01:13:30.860 --> 01:13:34.190
whereas if we could
incentivize camp residents

01:13:34.190 --> 01:13:36.770
or suburban residents,
we wanna call it that,

01:13:36.770 --> 01:13:39.210
to minimize kind of citizen crowd out

01:13:39.210 --> 01:13:41.540
and reduce expenditures on refugees,

01:13:41.540 --> 01:13:43.460
I think there's a better
model in here somewhere

01:13:43.460 --> 01:13:47.580
than just kind of pretending
that refugees in urban areas

01:13:47.580 --> 01:13:50.853
are going to be doing better just overall.

01:13:51.800 --> 01:13:53.180
And so we also wanna think

01:13:53.180 --> 01:13:54.980
about the kind of
counterfactual labor market

01:13:54.980 --> 01:13:57.600
and housing markets that matter here

01:13:57.600 --> 01:14:00.230
that those are going to be,
they're weak in this context,

01:14:00.230 --> 01:14:01.630
in the Jordanian context,

01:14:01.630 --> 01:14:04.540
whereas we think about this in Europe,

01:14:04.540 --> 01:14:05.950
that might be totally different

01:14:05.950 --> 01:14:08.150
and we wanna kind of
reevaluate this overall

01:14:09.350 --> 01:14:12.220
but it's just important
to kinda keep in mind

01:14:12.220 --> 01:14:14.530
we're thinking about generalizability

01:14:14.530 --> 01:14:17.750
and then as well as kind of
following the long-run outcomes

01:14:17.750 --> 01:14:20.900
but totally acknowledged
that these kind of outcomes

01:14:20.900 --> 01:14:24.470
are also are very uncertain
and just kind of call

01:14:24.470 --> 01:14:28.140
for more research in this area
to keep this discussion open

01:14:28.140 --> 01:14:30.720
around kind of what, what
arrangements could be better

01:14:30.720 --> 01:14:33.070
for both kind of host
communities and refugees.

01:14:34.260 --> 01:14:36.220
So I will leave it there.

01:14:36.220 --> 01:14:39.300
Thank you so much for, for
having me and for the questions

01:14:39.300 --> 01:14:42.240
and I'm happy to stick around with anyone

01:14:42.240 --> 01:14:43.700
that wants to, to chat more.

01:14:43.700 --> 01:14:44.653
So thanks again.

